{"entity":{"id":"hb:angevin-empire@1200","name":"Angevin Empire","dataset":"basemaps","kind":"empire"},"territories":[{"id":2140,"name":"Angevin Empire","set_id":"hb_1200","class":"empire","valid_from":1200,"valid_to":1242,"snapshot_held":1,"area_km2":260623.15122282787,"border_precision":1,"capital_name":null,"sovereign":null,"sovereign_note":null,"modern_name":"France","min_lon":-5.777813401715505,"min_lat":42.6078258382863,"max_lon":2.626750780051962,"max_lat":55.55607184048357,"source_file":"world_1200.geojson","source_row":null}],"identities":[{"qid":"Q538677","from_t":1200,"to_t":1242,"method":"name-label+date","confidence":0.95,"label":"Angevin Empire","description":"former collection of states in north-western Europe during the High Middle Ages","inception":1154,"dissolved":1242,"enwiki":"Angevin Empire","summary":"The Angevin Empire (; French: Empire Plantagenêt) was the collection of territories held by the House of Plantagenet during the 12th and 13th centuries, when they ruled over an area covering roughly all of present-day England, half of France, and parts of Ireland and Wales, and claiming overlordship and some influence over the remaining parts of Wales, Scotland and Ireland. It may be described as an early example of a composite monarchy. The empire was established by Henry II of England, who succeeded his father Geoffrey as duke of Normandy and count of Anjou (from the latter of which the term Angevin is derived). Henry married Eleanor of Aquitaine in 1152, acquiring the Duchy of Aquitaine, and inherited his mother Empress Matilda's claim to the English throne, succeeding his rival Stephen in 1154."}],"facts":[{"qid":"Q538677","kind":"official_language","value_qid":"Q35222","value_label":"Old French","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P37","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Old French"},{"qid":"Q538677","kind":"language","value_qid":"Q33850","value_label":"Norman","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P2936","retrieved":"2